  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kenku are mechanically solid, and excel in a variety of builds due to their excellent ability score increases and free skill proficiencies. They are defined more by their Dexterity increase than their Wisdom increase, and the Kenku is a go-to race for stealthy characters. With the custom origin rules or the updated version of the Kenku published in Monsters of the Multiverse, the Kenku is defined primarily by its skill proficiencies and Mimicry."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Thematically, the Kenku is a fantastically unique race with some interesting\n\tquirks. They are spectular imitators, but lack creativity. They can mimic\n\tsounds and ideas, but don’t have their own voices and can’t come up with plans\n\ton their own. This is an interesting roleplaying challenge; how do you play a\n\tgame that’s so dependent on clever thinking when your character can only\n\tregurgitate ideas which it heard elsewhere?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Of course, the updated version of the Kenku published in Monsters of the\n\tMultiverse discards much of that. While kenku are still fantastic at mimicry\n\tand imitation, WotC removed the Kenku’s inability to devise novel ideas or to\n\tspeak without mimicking other sounds and voices. There’s nothing to stop you\n\tfrom playing a kenku who relies entirely on mimicry, plagiarism, and\n\tduplication of other creatures’ behaviors, ideas, and speech, but it’s not\n\tlonger the expectation. That does remove some of the novelty of the race, but\n\tit also means that players might not feel as restrained in their actions when\n\tplaying a kenku."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
