        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-kenku-vs-tabaxi",
      "heading": "Kenku vs. Tabaxi",
      "sourceAnchor": "kenku-vs-tabaxi",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-kenku-vs-tabaxi-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Kenku and the Tabaxi have a lot of overlap. Their ability score increases\n\tare similar and they recieves similar skill proficiencies. However, the minor\n\tdifferences between the two leave some room for one to excel over the other\n\tdepending on the needs of the class or build."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-speech",
      "heading": "Kenku Speech",
      "sourceAnchor": "speech",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kenku’s unique method of speech is probably the most iconic feature of the\n\trace, and playing with this limitation draws many players to the Kenku over\n\tthe Tabaxi or the Wood Elf, which offer similar racial traits. However, many\n\tplayers misinterpret how the Kenku’s speech mechanics work."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kenku are capable of perfectly reproducing sounds which they hear, including\n\tvoices. There is no written limitation on how many sounds and voices you can\n\tremember and mimic, which means that any given kenku can build a nearly\n\tlimitless library of sounds to imitate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In an absolute worst-case scenario where a kenku has been exposed to\n\ttrivially small amounts of speech, they can either repeat words or short\n\tphrases in their entirety. Imagine only being able to speek in star wars\n\tprequel memes. Sure, you can respond to nearly any situation with one of the\n\tlines you know, but you lose a lot of nuance and specificity. This is the way\n\tmany players portray the Kenku’s Mimicry trait, and again: this is the\n\tabsolute worst-case scenario. A kenku which speaks this way has likely never\n\tmet a non-kenku capable of speech."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A more normal scenario would be a kenku who has heard several conversations\n\tbetween other speaking creatures over the course of their lifetime. Based on\n\treal-world research performed on speech patterns for natural language\n\tinterfaces like Siri and Alexa or for things like “deep fakes”, a computer can\n\treplicate the speech of a real human with reasonable accuracy based on\n\trecordings of\n\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "just a few seconds of speech",
              "href": "https://www.vice.com/en_us/article/3k7mgn/baidu-deep-voice-software-can-clone-anyones-voice-with-just-37-seconds-of-audio"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Kenku are natural mimics biologically predisposed to this capacity, and I\n\thave absolutely no doubt that a race of natural mimics could replicate natural\n\thuman speech after a hearing a few minutes of speech."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In extreme cases, kenku might learn speech in the same way that natural language interfaces do. Spoken language is composed of a collection of specific sounds rearranged in different orders to create sounds that we recognize as words. People whose voices are used as the basis for computerized speech (Siri, Alexa, etc.) participate in recording sessions where they record a library of these sounds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-6",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "These sounds are then arranged by computers to produce audible speech which can be adjusted in a few ways to sound like real speech. Kenku can do the same thing. A smart master could sit down with their kenku minions and read off a bunch of sounds, allowing kenku to speech almost naturally. Kenku could then perfectly mimic this teaching session, spreading the original master’s voice throughout the population."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-speech-7",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "However, even in the best of cases it’s not clear if kenku are creative\n\tenough to alter the inflection of their voices. A kenku which has been coached\n\twith a full range of sounds to replicate speech might still only be able to\n\tspeak in monotone, and kenku that assemble speech from a broad collection of\n\tsources might sound disjointed and fluxuate wildly in tone and pitch like an\n\told Speak & Spell. Individual families of kenku might all share one voice\n\twhich has been passed down for generations, allowing outsiders to identify\n\tregional kenku populations by their shared voices. These concepts aren’t\n\texplored in the official text, but it’s an interesting thought exercise, and\n\tit’s great fodder for roleplaying."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-kenku-versions",
      "heading": "Kenku Versions",
      "sourceAnchor": "kenku-versions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-kenku-versions-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "There are effectively three versions of the Kenku. The original was published\n\tin Volo’s Guide to Monsters. They were later reprinted in Explorer’s Guide to\n\tWildemount without any changes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-kenku-versions-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything introduced the option Customizing Your Origin\n\trules, which gave us the second version and allowing players to reassign the\n\tKenku’s ability score increases and removing the limitation on their two\n\tracial skill proficiencies."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-kenku-versions-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most recently, Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse re-published the Kenku, making major change to their lore and their racial traits. In addition to the standard changes to Ability Score Increases (everyone gets their choice of +2/+1 or three +1 increases), the Kenku’s other traits improved significantly. Kenku Training previously allowed 2 skills from a fixed list, but while other races went back to fixed proficiencies or a list of options, the Kenku now gets any two skills of their choice."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-kenku-versions-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kenku Training also offers an Advantage mechanic which makes the Kenku very effective at using skills. Mimicry was also updated to use a DC based on your Charisma rather than expecting you to be proficient in Deception in order to use it successfully. Altogether, this was a nice quality of life improvement to the Kenku, but not such a huge buff that it would cause problems."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-kenku-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "heading": "Kenku in the 2024 DnD Rules",
      "sourceAnchor": "kenku-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-kenku-in-the-2024-dnd-rules-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kenku were most recently updated in Monsters of the Multiverse, and have not yet been updated for the 2024 DnD rules. To play a Kenku in the 2024 DnD rules, ignore the Ability Score Increases described in Monsters of the Multiverse. Instead, use the Ability Score Increases provided by your Background in the 2024 rules."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Kenku Classes (Customizable Origins)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option updated versions of the race, including the “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything and/or the updated version published in "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Monsters of the Multiverse",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/motm/fantastical-races"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Because the race changed so little between those two versions, I have decided to combine these two sections. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the “Classic Rules” section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Kenku doesn’t bring anything unique to the Artificer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Tragically, Kenku Recall’s Advantage mechanic only applies to ability checks which use a skill, so you can’t use it with the Artificer’s abundant tool proficiencies."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kenku Recall’s Advantage mechanic can be helpful for Athletics checks to grapple when you can’t Rage for whatever reason, and two additional skills offer additional options to be useful outside of combat which the Barbarian sorely needs."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two skill proficiencies, the Advantage mechanic from Kenku Recall, and Mimicry all work very well for the Bard, allowing you to more easily cover multiple skill-based roles in the party. Combining with Expertise with Advantage from Kenku Recall makes it easy to be nearly unstoppable with skill checks without resorting to Enhance Ability constantly."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two skills offer some more options for the Cleric, but few clerics can do a lot with skills. Trickery Domain might allow you to take the place of a rogue in the party, and coupled with mimicry that feels thematically appropriate."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Druids don’t usually do a lot with skills, but since their armor options are poor many druids will have good enough Dexterity to also serve as a Scout if you take skill proficiencies similar to a rogue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two additional skills offer some sorely-needed options for the Fighter to be useful outside of combat. Strength-based builds can use Kenku Recall’s Advantage mechanic effectively for Athletics checks to grapple, and Dexterity-based builds can potentially serve as a Scout if you select the right proficiencies."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two skills and the Advantage mechanic from Kenku Recall (if you’re using the updated version of the Kenku) do a lot to close the skill gap between the Monk and the Rogue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two additional skill proficiencies will help you expand beyond Face skills. If you’re using the updated version of the Kenku, Kenku Recall offers a helpful Advantage option for important skill checks which paladins can’t replicate using spells or similar options."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two skills and the Advantage mechanic from Kenku recall do a lot to close the skill gap between the Ranger and the Rogue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two additional skills gives you even more options, and Kenku Recall works with any ability check where you proficiency in a skill applies, so your abundant skill proficiencies will give you even more opportunities to apply Kenku Recall. Just try not to blow through the limited uses too quickly."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two additional skill proficiencies will help you to expand beyond Face skills. If you’re using the updated version, the Kenku Recall’s Advantage mechanic is good, but not as good as Enhance Ability. Still, it may be enough that you might skip Enhance Ability or at least wait to get it a few levels longer than you might otherwise."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Two additional skill proficiencies will help you to expand beyond Face skills. Warlocks also can’t cast Enhance Ability, so the Kenku Recall’s Advantage mechanic is a decent substitute if you’re using the updated version of the Kenku."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "There simply aren’t enough Intelligence-based skills to make two additional skill proficiencies a major advantage for the Wizard. If you want Advantage, either have your familiar Help you or cast Enhance Ability."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Kenku Classes (Classic Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-default",
      "blocks": []
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bad ability spread."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bad abilty spread."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A Kenku bard is a really interesting concept, but without a Charisma increase\n\tyour core class features will lag behind other races."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dexterity and Wisdom are a decent combination for a cleric, and the Kenku’s\n\textra skill proficiencies can give you some extra capabilities simialr to a\n\trogue. A kenku trickery cleric would make excellent rogue replacement."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You get the critical Wisdom increase, and a Dexterity increase helps with the\n\tDruid’s notoriously terrible AC. The extra skill proficiencies don’t play\n\tespecially well to the Druid’s skillset, but like the Cleric you can easily\n\tfill in for a rogue with the right skills and background."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dexterity-based builds work really well, and the Kenku’s extra skills and\n\tunique flavor are great for a class which often feels dull when you’re not\n\tfighting."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dexterity and Wisdom are perfect for the Monk, and the Kenku’s additional\n\tskill proficiencies help the Monk to serve as a scout."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dexterity-based paladins are possible, but very little about the Kenku plays\n\tto the paladin mechanically or thematically."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Kenku are sneaky with Dexterity and Wisdom increases. You can hardly ask for\n\ta better ranger."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Rogue fits the Kenku very well thematically. The racial skill options give\n\tyou room to customize your build more than the Tabaxi, and the theme of the\n\trogue is absolutely perfect for a race suited to forgery, mimicry, and\n\tappropriation."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
